## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to add observability with OpenTelemetry in a docker-dca context, ensuring end-to-end trace propagation from edge to service and basic validation.

## Key Concepts
- OpenTelemetry instrumentation and OTLP exporter
- otel-collector as a sidecar and Jaeger backend
- Trace context propagation (traceparent/tracestate)
- docker-compose scaffolding for api, nginx, collector
- Basic end-to-end validation via Jaeger UI

## Code Example
```javascript
// Node.js OpenTelemetry setup (simplified)
const { NodeTracerProvider } = require('@opentelemetry/sdk-trace-node');
const { OTLPTraceExporter } = require('@opentelemetry/exporter-otlp-http');
const { SimpleSpanProcessor } = require('@opentelemetry/tracing');

const provider = new NodeTracerProvider();
provider.addSpanProcessor(new SimpleSpanProcessor(
  new OTLPTraceExporter({ url: 'http://otel-collector:4318/v1/traces' })
));
provider.register();
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adjust sampling and metric correlation to avoid perf impact on a busy API?
- How would you test trace failures or collector downtime without losing observability?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of docker-dca basics, per-tenant gating, and config management in real deployments across DCs.

## Key Concepts
- Docker Config for feature flags
- Hot reload of config without restart
- Tenant-scoped feature gating via header X-Tenant-ID
- TLS termination with Nginx and cross-DC routing

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal per-tenant feature gating middleware
const fs = require('fs');
let features = {};
function load(){
  try{ features = JSON.parse(fs.readFileSync('/config/flags.json','utf8')); }catch(e){ features = {}; }
}
load();
setInterval(load, 30000);
module.exports = function(req,res,next){
  const tenant = req.headers['x-tenant-id'];
  if (tenant && features[tenant] && features[tenant].includes('payments')) return next();
  res.status(403).send('Feature disabled');
};
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate config schema and handle partial reloads?
- How would you extend to multiple features and tenants with minimal latency impact?

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests the ability to design a tenant-scoped edge cache, ensure cross-DC coherence, and integrate with a docker-dca workflow including bootstrap commands and compose scaffolding.

## Key Concepts

- Per-tenant caching with tenant-scoped cache keys
- TTLs sourced from a central config service
- Cache invalidation on mutating requests and cross-DC coherence
- Edge proxy placement with TLS termination and header-based tenanting
- Observability and basic failure handling

## Code Example

```javascript
// Skeleton: per-tenant cache with TTL and invalidation broadcast
class TenantCache {
  constructor(configServiceUrl, pubSub) {
    this.cache = new Map();
    this.configServiceUrl = configServiceUrl;
    this.pubSub = pubSub;

    this.pubSub.subscribe('invalidate', (tenantId, path) => {
      this.invalidate(tenantId, path);
    });
  }

  async get(tenantId, path) {
    const ttl = await this.getTTL(tenantId);
    const key = `${tenantId}:${path}`;
    const entry = this.cache.get(key);

    if (entry && Date.now() < entry.expiresAt) {
      return entry.value;
    }
    return null;
  }

  async set(tenantId, path, value) {
    const ttl = await this.getTTL(tenantId);
    const key = `${tenantId}:${path}`;
    this.cache.set(key, {
      value,
      expiresAt: Date.now() + ttl
    });
  }

  async invalidate(tenantId, path) {
    const key = `${tenantId}:${path}`;
    this.cache.delete(key);
  }

  async broadcastInvalidation(tenantId, path) {
    await this.pubSub.publish('invalidate', tenantId, path);
  }

  async getTTL(tenantId) {
    // Fetch from central config service
    const config = await fetch(`${this.configServiceUrl}/tenants/${tenantId}`);
    return config.cacheTTL || 300;
  }
}
```

## Bootstrap Commands

```bash
# Build the sidecar image
docker build -t edge-cache-sidecar ./sidecar

# Deploy to docker-dca cluster
docker-dca deploy --file docker-compose.yml --service edge-cache-sidecar
```

## Docker Compose Skeleton

```yaml
version: '3.8'
services:
  api:
    image: your-api:latest
    ports:
      - "8080:8080"

  edge-cache:
    image: edge-cache-sidecar:latest
    ports:
      - "8081:8081"
    environment:
      - CONFIG_SERVICE_URL=http://config-service:8080
      - PUB_SUB_URL=redis://redis:6379
    depends_on:
      - api
      - redis

  nginx:
    image: nginx:latest
    ports:
      - "443:443"
    volumes:
      - ./nginx.conf:/etc/nginx/nginx.conf:ro
    depends_on:
      - edge-cache

  redis:
    image: redis:7-alpine

  config-service:
    image: config-service:latest
```

## Why This Is Asked
This tests practical reasoning on per-tenant quotas, config hot-reload via Docker Config, and lightweight canary routing in a docker-dca cluster.

## Key Concepts
- Docker Config for runtime config delivery
- Lightweight sidecar pattern for per-tenant quotas
- Token-bucket rate limiting per tenant
- Canary routing and rollback

## Code Example
```javascript
// Simplified quota sidecar sketch (pseudo)
const quotas = loadYamlFromConfig('quotas.yaml');
let buckets = {};

function handle(req, res, next){
  const tenant = req.headers['x-tenant'];
  if(!tenant) return res.status(400).send('Tenant required');
  const q = quotas[tenant] || quotas.default;
  const now = Date.now();
  let b = buckets[tenant] || { tokens: q.limit, last: now };
  const elapsed = Math.max(0, now - b.last);
  b.tokens = Math.min(q.limit, b.tokens + (elapsed * (q.refillRate || 1)));
  b.last = now;
  if(b.tokens < 1){ return res.status(429).send('Quota exceeded'); }
  b.tokens -= 1;
  buckets[tenant] = b;
  next();
}
```

